# rsyslog's files on sami-devbox's boot disk (dispatch://INFRA-5287). devbox/gce-host.sh copies this
# over the rsyslog package's /etc/logrotate.d/rsyslog, and installs devbox/logrotate-hourly.conf so
# that logrotate runs every hour.
#
# rsyslog writes a text copy of every record journald holds. The package rotates these files weekly
# and keeps four, with no size bound: on 2026-11-22 /var/log/syslog.1 held one week in 4.2 GiB
# (about 600 MiB a day, a third of it Postgres checkpoint lines), and /var/log held 7.3 GiB against
# sami-devbox-check's limit. Here a file rotates daily, and at the hourly run once it is over
# 200 MiB. 20 rotations are kept, gzip-compressed (15 to 1 on this box's syslog); the newest stays
# uncompressed until the next rotation, as in the package's file, since rsyslog writes to it until
# its HUP lands. So syslog takes at most about 250 MiB live, 250 MiB in syslog.1 and 19 x 17 MiB,
# under 1 GiB; at the volume of 2026-11-22, 20 rotations hold about a week.
/var/log/syslog
/var/log/mail.log
/var/log/kern.log
/var/log/auth.log
/var/log/user.log
/var/log/cron.log
{
	daily
	maxsize 200M
	rotate 20
	missingok
	notifempty
	compress
	delaycompress
	sharedscripts
	postrotate
		/usr/lib/rsyslog/rsyslog-rotate
	endscript
}
